Signals
A signal is a simple way of informing a process that some event has happened. It is mainly used for notifications rather than sending large amounts of data.

Examples of Signals
- SIGINT – Stops or interrupts a running process, usually when "Ctrl + C" is pressed.
- SIGTERM – Requests a process to terminate.
- SIGKILL – Immediately stops a process.
- SIGALRM – Used for timer or alarm events.

Advantages
- Simple and fast.
- Easy to use for notifications.
- Uses very little data.

Limitations
- Cannot be used to send large amounts of data.
- Only provides limited information about an event.


Message Brokers
A message broker is a system that helps different applications or services exchange messages. It works between the sender and receiver.

Basic Working

Sender → Message Broker → Receiver

For example, in an online shopping application, the order service can send a message to a message broker. The payment, inventory or delivery service can then receive that message.

Examples

- RabbitMQ
- Apache Kafka
- ActiveMQ
- Amazon SQS

Advantages

- Makes communication between services easier.
- Supports sending messages without waiting for the receiver.
- Useful when many applications need to communicate.
- Can store messages until they are processed.

Comparison of IPC Methods

IPC Method| Main Use| Speed| Example
Pipes| Simple data transfer| Fast| Parent and child process
Named Pipes| Communication between processes| Fast| Local client-server
Message Queues| Sending messages| Medium| Task processing
Shared Memory| Sharing large data| Very Fast| Image/video processing
Memory-Mapped Files| Sharing file data| Fast| File sharing
Semaphores & Mutexes| Synchronization| Very Fast| Protecting shared data
Sockets| Network communication| Medium| Web applications
RPC| Calling a function remotely| Medium| Distributed applications
Signals| Sending notifications| Very Fast| Process control
Message Brokers| Communication between services| Depends on system| Microservices

Each method is useful for a different purpose. For example, shared memory is useful for large amounts of data, signals are useful for notifications, and sockets are useful when communication is needed over a network.


Real-World Applications of IPC

IPC is used in many applications and operating systems.

1. Operating Systems

Different processes running in an operating system use IPC to communicate and share information.

2. Web Browsers

Different parts of a browser, such as the browser process and rendering process, communicate with each other using IPC.

3. Banking Applications

Banking systems have different services for accounts, payments and transactions. IPC helps these services communicate.

4. Online Shopping

When a customer places an order, different services such as payment, inventory and delivery need to communicate.

5. Cloud Applications

Cloud applications often have many small services. Message brokers, sockets and RPC can be used for communication between these services.

6. Multimedia Applications

Shared memory can be used when processes need to quickly share large amounts of image, audio or video data.


Conclusion

IPC is an important part of operating systems because it allows different processes to communicate and work together.

There are many IPC methods, and each one has its own purpose. Signals are useful for notifications, shared memory is useful for fast data sharing, sockets are useful for network communication, and message brokers are useful for communication between different services.

Choosing the right IPC method depends on the type of communication and the requirements of the application.
